      "timeframe": "1min",
      "tags": [
        "ema",
        "crossover",
        "momentum",
        "trend-following",
        "intraday"
      ],
      "thesis": "EMA(9) crossing above EMA(21) on 1-min bars signals a short-term momentum shift. The 9-bar EMA captures the last ~9 minutes of sentiment while the 21-bar EMA captures the last ~21 minutes. A cross indicates that short-term buying pressure has overtaken medium-term average price, often signaling the ",
      "indicators": [
        "EMA_9",
        "EMA_21",
        "ADX_14",
        "VWAP",
        "ATR_14"
      ],
      "entry_long": [
        "EMA(close, 9) > EMA(close, 21)",
        "EMA(close, 9)[t-1] <= EMA(close, 21)[t-1]",
        "ADX(14) > 25",
        "close > VWAP",
        "volume > SMA(volume, 20)"
      ],
      "entry_short": [
        "EMA(close, 9) < EMA(close, 21)",
        "EMA(close, 9)[t-1] >= EMA(close, 21)[t-1]",
        "ADX(14) > 25",
        "close < VWAP",
        "volume > SMA(volume, 20)"
      ],
      "exit_target": "0.4% from entry",
      "exit_stop": "0.2% from entry OR EMA(9) re-crosses EMA(21) in opposite direction",
      "filters": [
        "no specific filter \u2014 momentum works across VIX regimes",
        "no entries before 09:30 or after 14:45",
        "volume on cross bar > 1x SMA(volume, 20)",
        "ADX(14) > 25 (only trade in trending conditions)",
        "bid-ask spread < 0.05%"
      ],
      "weaknesses": [
        "Win rate is low (~42%) \u2014 requires psychological discipline to endure losing streaks of 5-8 trades",
        "EMA crossover on 1-min bars generates many false signals even with ADX filter \u2014 choppy days accumulate losses",
        "The signal is extremely well-known and taught in every beginner trading course \u2014 crowding risk is high"
      ],
      "prior_art": "EMA crossover is perhaps the most widely known trading signal (Murphy, 'Technical Analysis'). ADX as a trend strength filter is from Wilder (1978). The 9/21 EMA pair is commonly used by retail traders"
    },
