      "thesis": "EMA(9) / EMA(21) crossover signals on 1-min bars in range-bound markets are reliably faded because the crossover lags the actual move by 3-5 bars, meaning late momentum chasers enter at the worst price. By fading these crossovers when the stock is in a defined range (ATR_60 < 1.5 * median ATR_60 ove",
      "indicators": [
        "ema_9",
        "ema_21",
        "ema_cross_up",
        "ema_cross_down",
        "atr_60",
        "atr_60_median_week",
        "atr_20"
      ],
      "entry_long": [
        "ema_cross_down == True (bearish cross just fired)",
        "atr_60 < 1.5 * atr_60_median_week",
        "close > vwap * 0.995"
      ],
      "entry_short": [
        "ema_cross_up == True (bullish cross just fired)",
        "atr_60 < 1.5 * atr_60_median_week",
        "close < vwap * 1.005"
      ],
      "exit_target": "0.3% from entry or ema_9 reverts back to pre-crossover side",
      "exit_stop": "0.35% from entry",
      "filters": [
        "none \u2014 range filter handles regime",
        "no trades first 15 min or last 30 min",
        "skip if volume < 60% of 20-bar avg",
        "atr_60 < 1.5 * weekly median (range-bound filter)",
        "skip if spread > 0.1%"
      ],
      "weaknesses": [
        "In genuine breakout days, fading the crossover gets destroyed \u2014 the range filter mitigates but doesn't eliminate",
        "The 1950-bar lookback for weekly median requires significant warmup data each morning",
        "2-bar wait for confirmation reduces trade frequency and sometimes means the reversion already happened"
      ],
      "prior_art": "Fading EMA crossovers in range-bound conditions \u2014 contrarian take on a classic retail signal"
    },
    {
